---
import { waHref } from '../data/whatsapp.js';
// VISUAILS — /ai-act. Rewritten 3 August 2026 from copy supplied by Lucas,
// which replaces the July 2026 version wholesale. The old page argued about
// whether the disclosure rule reached ordinary product imagery; that argument
// is over (see §2 below), so a page built on it would now be wrong rather than
// careful.
//
// WHAT WAS DELIBERATELY CUT FROM THE SUPPLIED COPY, AND WHY
//
//   "Drie beeldvarianten bij elke levering" — a table promising every delivery
//   ships in three cuts (unlabelled / discrete badge / embedded 50% notice).
//   Cut on Lucas's instruction because none of it exists: `files` has one
//   `kind` column (upload | delivery | thumbnail) and no variant concept, and
//   there is no delivery endpoint yet at all. A transparency page that opens by
//   promising a deliverable that does not exist has defeated itself in its own
//   first table. §3 now lists the three things that DO exist. If the variants
//   get built, this is the section that gains a fourth entry — not the other
//   way round.
//
//   §4 "De gezichten" from the previous version — the shared standard library
//   against a Brand Model. Cut on Lucas's instruction: it is commercial
//   information for a brand choosing between the ten standard faces and a paid
//   exclusive one, not an AI Act matter, and /models is where a buyer meets
//   that choice. FLAGGED: it is now on no page in this form. It is worth
//   saying somewhere that a standard face can appear on another brand.
//
// WHAT WAS CORRECTED IN THE SUPPLIED COPY
//
//   1. "Beelden die je vóór 2 augustus 2026 van ons kreeg" → the trigger is the
//      date the image was GENERATED, not the date it was received or published.
//      The final Guidelines are explicit that images and video turn on the
//      generation date while text turns on the publication date, so a delivery
//      date is the wrong hinge and would be wrong in the client's favour.
//   2. "Sinds 2 augustus 2026 geldt een vermeldingsplicht voor AI-beeld dat een
//      echt product toont" → kept as the message, anchored to what actually
//      carries it: the Guidelines treat marketing imagery that makes a product
//      look different from reality as a deep fake under Art. 50(4), the duty
//      sits on whoever publishes, and the creative-work exception is read
//      strictly. Stating the duty without stating who carries it was the one
//      place the supplied copy risked speaking for the reader's business.
//   3. The provenance claim — see the block below. This is the biggest change.
//
// THE PROVENANCE CLAIM, AND WHY §6 IS SO SPECIFIC ABOUT IT
//
// The supplied copy said the machine-readable marking "komt van de
// modelaanbieders, wij verwijderen die niet", full stop. Lucas confirmed the
// jpg and png deliverables carry it and flagged webp as unknown because the
// conversion is done ad hoc. Tested rather than assumed, 3 August 2026:
//
//   - A jpeg carrying an APP11/JUMBF segment (how C2PA sits in a jpeg),
//     converted to webp with sharp — the encoder this project already uses in
//     scripts/make-thumbs.mjs — comes out as a bare 'VP8 ' chunk. The payload
//     is gone. With .withMetadata() the output gains 'ICCP' and 'EXIF' chunks
//     and the C2PA payload is STILL gone: sharp carries colour profile and EXIF,
//     it does not carry a JUMBF box into webp's C2PA RIFF chunk.
//   - Every .webp in public/img was then checked the same way. All of them are
//     a single 'VP8 ' chunk — no EXIF, no XMP, no C2PA. The site's own imagery
//     demonstrates the finding.
//
// webp is not the problem — the C2PA spec supports it, in a 'C2PA' RIFF chunk.
// Generic converters are the problem: preserving a manifest across a re-encode
// takes a C2PA-aware tool, and neither sharp nor Pillow is one. So §6 says which
// formats carry it and which do not, and tells the reader not to rely on the
// file to identify itself. That sentence is worth more to a brand than the
// blanket reassurance it replaces, and unlike the blanket version it is true.
//
// FACTS VERIFIED 3 AUGUST 2026 against the Commission's own pages and the
// firm commentary on the final texts:
//   - Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 in force 1 Aug 2024; Art. 50 applies 2 Aug 2026.
//   - Final Guidelines on transparency of AI-generated content published
//     20 July 2026; the Code of Practice on marking and labelling was confirmed
//     adequate by the Commission and the AI Board and applies from 2 Aug 2026.
//   - Art. 50(2) machine-readable marking: grace to 2 Dec 2026 for generative
//     systems already on the market before 2 Aug 2026. This is why §6 says not
//     every model marks yet — it is a live transitional fact, not hedging.
//   - The human-review exemption in Art. 50(4) covers TEXT on matters of public
//     interest. It does not reach images. The supplied copy had this right and
//     it is repeated here because it is the single most common thing a supplier
//     gets wrong in a client's favour.
//   - The Code of Practice carries a set of icons a deployer may use. They are
//     an aid, not a mark of conformity, and the supplied copy had that right too.
// Nothing here names the Digital Omnibus: it does not touch Art. 50 for this
// use, and naming an instrument a reader cannot act on is noise on a trust page.
import { localizedPath } from '../i18n/ui.js';
import FigAnatomy from './FigAnatomy.astro';
import Disclose from './Disclose.astro';

interface Props { lang: 'en' | 'nl'; }
const { lang } = Astro.props;
const lp = (p: string) => localizedPath(lang, p);

// No `as const`: COPY[lang] would resolve to a union of readonly tuple arrays
// and .map() on that union is a TS error. Every page component omits it.
