    </ul>

    {/* FROM HERE DOWN THE PAGE FOLDS. §1–§4 are the message — what the rules
        are aimed at, what you get, and why the image has to match the product —
        and every reader needs those to decide anything. §5–§8 are looked up
        rather than read: what to do at publication, what we refuse to claim,
        who carries what, and where we sit. DESIGN.md's disclosure rule is
        exactly this split, and the page went from 1,567 words in one column to
        four lines plus the argument.

        Not one shared `name`: these four are reference, and somebody checking
        their responsibilities against what we do not claim wants both open at
        once. The exclusive accordion is for step 1, where the whole point is
        one control at a time. */}
    <div class="legal-folds">
      <Disclose summary={c.s5H} tone="plain">
